WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.610
RETO MEIER: Today, we're
talking about machine images,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.280
deployment pipelines,
and the dashboard API.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.800
I'm Reto Meier, and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.100
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.100
Compute Engine now
offers machine images.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.800
Machine images contain all
the instance configurations

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.110
and metadata for
your virtual machine.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.150
That means creating,
backing up, and restoring

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.520
virtual machines is
easier than ever,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.670
even if you use custom images,
because properties like machine

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.670
type, network tags, and
labels, and so on are all

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.009
captured in a single resource.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.140
Google Kubernetes Engine
just got an upgrade

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.120
with automated
deployments that require

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.460
no management or updates.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.350
The pipelines are
powered by Cloud Build

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.910
and run automatically
when changes

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.810
are made to the source code.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.210
You can even
preview a deployment

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.370
from your pull request
to verify the change will

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.870
work as expected.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.470
Try it for yourself, and
let us know what you think.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.180
The Cloud Monitoring
Dashboard's API

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.730
was released last month to
help you create dashboards

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.160
programmatically.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.020
A practical use case here is
taking an existing dashboard

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.570
and using the API to
recreate it across all

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.250
of your other workspaces.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.680
And that's exactly why one
of our solutions architects

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.100
created this walkthrough.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.810
Migrating a monolith
is best done in stages.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.030
To see why that's true
and how to best move

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.540
through those stages,
check out this tutorial.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.830
It covers how to migrate
using Google Kubernetes

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.620
Engine from prep work
to dependency analysis

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.660
to real-time migration advice.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.097
